The standout discovery for your particular architecture is that **World Bank now publishes an official Data360 MCP server**. It lets agents search indicators, inspect metadata/disaggregations, retrieve time series, and work with structured development data instead of an LLM inventing numbers. ([GitHub][1])

### 1. World Bank should be your statistical spine

Clone:

```bash
git clone https://github.com/worldbank/data360-mcp
git clone https://github.com/worldbank/pipr
```

Data360 provides the broad world-state information. The World Bank also exposes the underlying Data360 API. ([World Bank Open Data][2])

PIP is more important than just GDP because it lets you ask:

> What percentage of Bangladeshis consume less than $10/day PPP?

> Where is someone earning $600/month in the global distribution?

> How has the bottom 20% changed?

The official `pipr` client accesses household-survey-derived poverty and inequality estimates for 160+ countries and regions. ([GitHub][3])

That's **Unignorant/MONEY**.

---

# 2. HDX is probably your biggest raw-data goldmine

This is enormous for the project.

Clone:

```bash
git clone https://github.com/OCHA-DAP/hdx-python-api
```

OCHA maintains the official Python API for HDX. ([GitHub][4])

It gives you a programmatic route into datasets concerning things like:

* food insecurity
* humanitarian needs
* displacement
* infrastructure
* health
* administrative boundaries
* population
* conflict-affected areas
* crisis response

There is even a working dashboard example using the Humanitarian API that displays population, humanitarian needs, food insecurity and operational presence. ([OCHA DAP][5])

And someone has already wrapped HDX in MCP:

```bash
git clone https://github.com/dividor/hdx-mcp
```

It exposes HDX queries as tools for AI assistants. ([GitHub][6])

That's nearly plug-and-play for an Unignorant research agent.

---

# 3. Open Supply Hub is ridiculously relevant

This is probably your most important corporate/supply-chain repository.

```bash
git clone https://github.com/opensupplyhub/open-supply-hub
git clone https://github.com/opensupplyhub/pyoshub
git clone https://github.com/opensupplyhub/open-supply-hub-api-examples
```

The main app is open source. ([GitHub][7])

They explicitly publish a Python library for their API. ([GitHub][8])

And the examples repository contains integration documentation and test material. API access requires obtaining a token. ([GitHub][9])

This gives you:

```text
Brand
   ↓
Supplier
   ↓
Facility
   ↓
lat/lon
   ↓
Industry
   ↓
Local community
```

Then **you** enrich the facility.

```text
facility
 ├─ OS Hub
 ├─ local World Bank poverty
 ├─ ILO wages
 ├─ OSM nearby housing/infrastructure
 ├─ GDELT news
 ├─ corporate allegations
 └─ trade flows
```

That begins turning a pin representing a factory into **a piece of lived reality**.

---

# 4. ILOSTAT tells you what humans actually do all day

Official ILO tooling exists:

```bash
git clone https://github.com/ilostat/rilostat
```

ILOSTAT covers essentially the labour questions you need: employment, unemployment, wages, working time, labour productivity and many other labour indicators internationally. ([GitHub][10])

Someone has even built a tiny LLM summarizer over ILOSTAT:

```bash
git clone https://github.com/justintemps/ilostat-simple-summarizer
```

It uses AI to summarize ILO labour statistics. ([GitHub][11])

Don't clone its product. Clone the idea:

```text
ILOSTAT observation
        ↓
deterministic calculations
        ↓
LLM explanation
        ↓
"What this means in ordinary life"
```

That's **Unignorant/WORK**.

---

# 5. UN Comtrade lets you reconstruct the physical global economy

This is critical.

Official:

```bash
git clone https://github.com/uncomtrade/comtradeapicall
```

It is maintained specifically to simplify calls to the UN Comtrade APIs. ([GitHub][12])

Also very useful:

```bash
git clone https://github.com/harvard-growth-lab/comtrade-downloader
```

Harvard Growth Lab's implementation handles:

* bilateral trade downloads
* commodity classifications
* classification conversion
* incremental updates
* aggregation

which saves you building boring ingestion machinery yourself. ([GitHub][13])

Now Unignorant can answer:

> Where does Britain's coffee actually come from?

> Who exports Cambodia's clothes?

> Which countries supply cobalt to China?

> Where do Bangladesh's garments go?

Conceptually:

```text
mine
 ↓
commodity
 ↓
exporter
 ↓
importer
 ↓
manufacturer
 ↓
brand
 ↓
British consumer
```

**Comtrade gives you the middle part of that graph.**

---

# 6. GDELT gives you “what is happening there right now”

GDELT describes itself as a real-time database/network representation of global society based on worldwide news. ([GDELT Project][14])

Its geographic datasets alone contain **over 1.6 billion location mentions** from international news coverage. ([GDELT Project][15])

For easy Python usage:

```bash
git clone https://github.com/alex9smith/gdelt-doc-api
```

That package wraps the GDELT DOC API. ([GitHub][16])

But this might be even better for your agent architecture:

```bash
git clone https://github.com/cyanheads/gdelt-mcp-server
```

It exposes **nine MCP tools** for searching/analyzing GDELT global news. ([GitHub][17])

There is also:

```bash
git clone https://github.com/shakydata/gdelt-cli
```

which is explicitly built as an agent-friendly CLI over GDELT. ([GitHub][18])

Someone has already demonstrated an ELT pipeline processing **100,000+ global news events per day**:

```bash
git clone https://github.com/Mohith-akash/Global-News-Intel-Platform
```

([GitHub][19])

I would absolutely inspect that ingestion architecture.

---

# 7. ReliefWeb is much cleaner than scraping humanitarian news

This has a proper JSON API:

```text
https://api.reliefweb.int/v1
```

The API exposes ReliefWeb's curated, continuously updated archive and is used by ReliefWeb itself and third-party applications. ([Reliefweb API][20])

Existing Go client:

```bash
git clone https://github.com/reliefweb/api-go-client
```

([GitHub][21])

So:

```text
GDELT
= huge global news firehose

ReliefWeb
= curated humanitarian reporting

HDX
= actual underlying crisis datasets
```

Use all three.

---

# 8. IATI answers “WHERE THE CHARITY/AID MONEY ACTUALLY WENT”

This one is perfect for something you asked earlier.

International Aid Transparency Initiative data contains development activities and transactions from governments, multilaterals, NGOs and other organizations.

Their current datastore can be queried and exported as **JSON, CSV or XML**, and is dynamically updated from the IATI Registry. ([IATI Standard][22])

Open implementations:

```bash
git clone https://github.com/IATI/IATI-Datastore
git clone https://github.com/zimmerman-team/IATI.cloud
```

IATI.cloud ingests published IATI XML into Apache Solr for fast querying. ([GitHub][23])

This gives you something amazing:

```text
UK government
   ↓ £50m
NGO/programme
   ↓
country
   ↓
sector
   ↓
project
   ↓
transactions
```

Eventually Unignorant could have:

# WHERE DOES FOREIGN AID ACTUALLY GO?

rather than people endlessly arguing from vague impressions.

---

# 9. WorldPop + HOT/OSM gives you the physical world people inhabit

WorldPop has a large open GitHub organization and tooling for high-resolution population distributions. ([GitHub][24])

Convenient unofficial Python layer:

```bash
git clone https://github.com/lungoruscello/worldpoppy
```

It handles downloading/merging WorldPop rasters for an arbitrary area of interest. ([GitHub][25])

Combine with HOT's excellent APIs:

```bash
git clone https://github.com/hotosm/raw-data-api
git clone https://github.com/hotosm/osm-export-tool
```

The Raw Data API turns OpenStreetMap data into GIS exports programmatically. ([GitHub][26])

That means around a poor community you can actually ask:

```text
population within 5 km
roads
schools
hospitals/clinics
water infrastructure
shops
buildings
industrial facilities
```

This is far richer than saying:

> “Province poverty rate: 38%.”

---

# 10. DHS is where “what households actually live like” gets serious

This may eventually become one of your most valuable sources.

```bash
git clone https://github.com/ropensci/rdhs
```

DHS has conducted population surveys across **90+ countries over decades**, including household/health information. ([GitHub][27])

Depending on dataset/access conditions, you can get variables around:

* household assets
* water source
* toilet
* electricity
* children
* education
* maternal health
* nutrition
* mortality
* housing

This is much closer to:

> **what actual humans live like**

than country-level macro data.

IPUMS can also harmonize many forms of microdata:

```bash
git clone https://github.com/ipums/ipumsr
```

including international census and health survey collections. ([GitHub][28])

Some microdata requires registration/approval, so don't design your core public pipeline assuming unrestricted redistribution.

---

# 11. Conflict: ACLED

Useful packages already exist:

```bash
git clone https://github.com/datapartnership/acled_conflict_analysis
```

It performs extraction, processing, aggregation and visualization over ACLED conflict events. ([GitHub][29])

Official R access also exists. ([GitHub][30])

There's a particularly clever idea already documented by Bellingcat:

> **Conflict Exposure Calculator**

It combines ACLED events with WorldPop population estimates to estimate civilian exposure to conflict. ([GitHub][31])

**That mechanism is worth stealing conceptually.**

Instead of:

> 14 conflict events occurred.

Unignorant says:

> approximately X people live within Y km of recorded conflict events.

Much more human.

---

# 12. Corporate ownership is harder, but there are pieces

OpenCorporates has APIs, though access/licensing is less frictionless than the public UN/World Bank datasets. Existing wrappers and pipelines demonstrate the approach. ([GitHub][32])

This repo is especially interesting:

```bash
git clone https://github.com/StephenAbbott/bods-opencorporates
```

It transforms OpenCorporates data into **Beneficial Ownership Data Standard** statements:

```text
company
 ↕
officer/person
 ↕
ownership/control
 ↕
company
```

([GitHub][33])

That could eventually give Unignorant a genuine corporate graph rather than a list of brands.

---

## And BHRRC?

**Business & Human Rights Resource Centre is one of the best content datasets for you but one of the less clean ingestion targets.**

Its platform contains ongoing news, allegations and disclosure material concerning **20,000+ companies**. ([Myanmar Centre for Responsible Business][34])

I didn't find a clearly documented general-purpose public API comparable to HDX/ReliefWeb/Data360 in this search.

So I would classify it:

> **scrape/feed/partnership candidate, not foundational API**

And don't indiscriminately scrape it before checking its terms, robots rules and content licensing.

This distinction matters.

