# What I'd clone first

If I were starting the repo tonight:

```bash
mkdir vendor && cd vendor

git clone https://github.com/worldbank/data360-mcp
git clone https://github.com/OCHA-DAP/hdx-python-api
git clone https://github.com/dividor/hdx-mcp
git clone https://github.com/opensupplyhub/pyoshub
git clone https://github.com/opensupplyhub/open-supply-hub-api-examples
git clone https://github.com/uncomtrade/comtradeapicall
git clone https://github.com/harvard-growth-lab/comtrade-downloader
git clone https://github.com/cyanheads/gdelt-mcp-server
git clone https://github.com/shakydata/gdelt-cli
git clone https://github.com/datapartnership/acled_conflict_analysis
git clone https://github.com/hotosm/raw-data-api
git clone https://github.com/ropensci/rdhs
```

Not necessarily as permanent dependencies. **Read their schemas, API clients, pagination, caching and normalization approaches.**

The four I'd actually integrate first are:

**Data360 → baseline reality**
**ILOSTAT → work**
**Open Supply Hub → production**
**GDELT/ReliefWeb → what is changing now**

Then:

**Comtrade → supply-chain flows**
**HDX → humanitarian conditions**
**IATI → money/aid**
**WorldPop/OSM → spatial reality**

And only *after that* start scraping journalistic/accountability sources.

The important realization from this search is that **the data-collection problem is substantially more solved than it initially appeared**. You don't need to invent a database of Earth. Governments, UN agencies, NGOs and researchers have already published surprisingly large fragments of it. The interesting engineering problem is **entity resolution + provenance + joining those fragments into one human-readable world graph.** ([GitHub][1])

I can also track newly released open datasets/repos relevant to Unignorant.

